{"id":"R0001","corrections":{"E4":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Section III.A and Figs. 2-3 on p. 3 evaluate the proposed algorithm (named CAPRI on p. 1 and CAPRIC on p. 3) in a simulation setting with 10 UAVs, 4 edge servers, and one cloud server using a public drone delivery dataset and a real base station location dataset against seven baselines (local, DQN, cloud_only, no_local, edge_only, end_edge, no_energy). The reported evaluation metric is reward (Fig. 2: Reward convergence; Fig. 3: Rewards on a); no plotted points are estimated, and omitting separate raw latency/energy tables is a reporting limitation rather than an E4 failure under P03.","evidence":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0001.pdf","page":3,"locator":"Section III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"considers 10 UAVs, 4 ESs and one cloud server."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0001.pdf","page":3,"locator":"Section III.A, Fig. 2","quote":"where CAPRIC outperforms all other baseline methods."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0001.pdf","page":3,"locator":"Section III.A, Fig. 3","quote":"CAPRIC achieves the highest reward across all configurations"}],"gap":null},"E7":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"The official ICWS 2026 program lists this report as WIP paper CWS_WIP_222 in session CWS-WIP-S3, and the official IEEE SERVICES 2026 Info for Authors policy confirms WIP papers (up to 3 pages of main content plus references) undergo double-blind peer review by at least three program committee members for IEEE Computer Society Press proceedings. Under P03 E7, a 3-page short/WIP conference paper is eligible because peer review is verified and the PDF defines the inference method (pp. 1-3) and reports evaluation results with identifiable conditions and reward metric (p. 3); lack of raw latency/energy tables is a study limitation, not E7 ineligibility.","evidence":[{"source":"https://services.conferences.computer.org/2026/icws-program/","page":null,"locator":"CWS-WIP-S3: Microservices","quote":"CWS_WIP_222: Adaptive Partition and Resource Allocation for Collaborative Inference"},{"source":"https://services.conferences.computer.org/2026/info-for-authors/","page":null,"locator":"Information for Authors, Peer Review","quote":"peer-reviewed by at least three members of the program committee."},{"source":"https://services.conferences.computer.org/2026/info-for-authors/","page":null,"locator":"Information for Authors, Page limits","quote":"up to 3 pages of the main contents"}],"gap":null}},"recommendation":"KEEP","primary_code":null}
{"id":"R0113","corrections":{"E2":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"The report explicitly evaluates a multi-UAV setting in simulation, specifying a swarm of 40 cooperative UAVs in Implementation and Results (p. 9) and testing increasing swarm sizes of 10, 25, 50, and 100 UAVs in the scalability analysis (p. 12, Table 3).","evidence":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0113.pdf","page":9,"locator":"Implementation and Results","quote":"a simulated autonomous UAV swarm consisting of 40 cooperative UAVs"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0113.pdf","page":12,"locator":"Table 3 caption","quote":"Scalability analysis of autonomous UAV swarm navigation with increasing swarm"}],"gap":null},"E3":{"status":"UNRESOLVED","finding":"The methodology states that UAVs exchange compact navigation embeddings via graph message passing (p. 7) fused with SNN representations, and p. 9 states Graph Neural Networks managed swarm communication, with p. 12 Table 3 and p. 13 Table 4 reporting an SNN + CL + Graph Coordination ablation. However, there is a missing link: neither the methodology nor the evaluation defines the SNN or GNN architectures, embedding dimensions, message-passing equations, or how neural inference features are actually distributed or fused across UAVs in the reported runs, preventing conclusive verification under P03 E3.","evidence":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0113.pdf","page":7,"locator":"Methodology","quote":"Each UAV periodically exchanges compact navigation embeddings, local obstacle information"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0113.pdf","page":9,"locator":"Implementation and Results","quote":"Graph Neural Networks managed swarm communication and reinforcement learning optimized"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0113.pdf","page":12,"locator":"Implementation and Results, Ablation Study","quote":"SNN with Continual Learning and Graph-Based Swarm Coordination"}],"gap":"Missing architectural and experimental specification linking the claimed p. 7 navigation embeddings and p. 9 GNN to concrete collaborative DNN inference execution or feature fusion in the reported evaluation."},"E4":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Implementation and Results (pp. 9-14) report simulation and hardware-in-the-loop evaluations comparing the proposed NSNN-CL framework against CNN, Vision Transformer, Conventional SNN, and continual-learning baselines plus scalability and ablation studies across Tables 1-4 and Figs. 1-4. While experimental protocols, metric definitions, and Fig. 3 plotting consistency are severely under-specified (appraisal limitations), evaluative results are reported under P03 E4.","evidence":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0113.pdf","page":9,"locator":"Implementation and Results","quote":"performed in both high-fidelity simulation environments and hardware-in-the-loop (HIL)"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0113.pdf","page":10,"locator":"Table 1 caption","quote":"Performance comparison of autonomous UAV swarm navigation using different"}],"gap":null},"E7":{"status":"UNRESOLVED","finding":"The original PDF masthead labels AJACCM a peer-reviewed journal with article history Received 02-01-2026, Accepted 05-02-2026, Published 12-02-2026 (p. 1), and the journal website hosts the article (view/771) plus Author Guidelines and Policies pages asserting peer review. However, the incomplete DOI prefix and advertised 1-2 day acceptance alone neither prove nor disprove peer review, and the journal's own policy pages conflict internally (Author Guidelines states double-blind review by at least two independent experts while referencing IJDIM; Policies states review by at least one external reviewer while referencing AJMIMC), leaving peer-review status unresolved without speculating on authenticity.","evidence":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0113.pdf","page":1,"locator":"Footer article history","quote":"Received: 02-01-2026 | Accepted: 05-02-2026 | Published: 12-02-2026"},{"source":"https://ajaccm.com/journal/index.php/ajaccm/author-guidelines","page":null,"locator":"Section 8. Peer Review Process","quote":"AJACCM follows a double-blind peer review process"},{"source":"https://ajaccm.com/journal/index.php/ajaccm/policies","page":null,"locator":"Peer Review Policy","quote":"reviewed by at least one external reviewer"}],"gap":"Internal policy inconsistencies across AJACCM Author Guidelines and Policies (conflicting minimum reviewer counts and mismatched journal names IJDIM/AJMIMC) and lack of independent publisher/Crossref verification leave peer-review status unresolved."}},"recommendation":"UNRESOLVED","primary_code":null}
{"id":"R0145","corrections":{"E3":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Under P03 E3, explicit UAV-to-cloud assignment of full LLM inference requests qualifies without direct inter-UAV coordination or layer splitting. In a 10-UAV swarm with one cloud large LLM and per-UAV small quantized LLMs (p. 6 Section 3.1), each UAV dynamically assigns full motion-inference requests to the cloud large LLM with expanded perception when UAV-cloud communication is good (zeta=1) or executes locally on its onboard small LLM with reduced perception when communication is poor or unavailable (zeta=0) (pp. 2, 6, 12).","evidence":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0145.pdf","page":2,"locator":"Section 1 Introduction","quote":"upload the perceived information to the cloud for motion inference"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0145.pdf","page":6,"locator":"Section 3.1 System Overview","quote":"one is a large LLM deployed in the cloud."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0145.pdf","page":12,"locator":"Section 5.1 UAV Swarm Path Planning","quote":"generate movement decisions by using its own deployed small LLM"}],"gap":null},"E4":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Separately from the model-selection component benchmarks in Figs. 4-6 and traditional-method comparisons in Section 6.3, Section 6.2 (pp. 16-18, Figs. 7-10) evaluates three cloud-edge assignment schemes on a fixed 10-UAV scenario: large LLM only (good communication), hybrid LLM (mixed cloud-connected and local UAVs), and small LLM only (very poor communication), reporting communication time, inference time, computational cost (GFLOPs), total runtime, and decision steps.","evidence":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0145.pdf","page":17,"locator":"Section 6.2, Figs. 7-9","quote":"large LLM only, hybrid LLM, and small LLM only."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0145.pdf","page":17,"locator":"Section 6.2, Figs. 7-9","quote":"compared the communication time, inference time, and computational cost"}],"gap":null},"E7":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"ACM's official AI Journals catalog explicitly identifies ACM Transactions on Autonomous and Adaptive Systems (TAAS) as an ACM peer-reviewed periodical, governed by the ACM Peer Review Policy, and the original PDF establishes full-text journal acceptance and revision history (Received 23 June 2025; revised 6 September 2025; accepted 22 October 2025, p. 22; Just Accepted, DOI 10.1145/3774655, p. 1). Direct Crossref API and ACM Digital Library article pages were inaccessible in this session, so verification rests on the opened official ACM publisher pages and PDF history.","evidence":[{"source":"https://www.acm.org/publications/ai-journals","page":null,"locator":"AI Journals from ACM","quote":"ACM's peer-reviewed periodicals in the field of Artificial Intelligence"},{"source":"https://www.acm.org/publications/policies/peer-review","page":null,"locator":"Purpose and Scope","quote":"built on a foundation of high-quality peer review."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0145.pdf","page":22,"locator":"Article history footer","quote":"Received 23 June 2025; revised 6 September 2025"}],"gap":null}},"recommendation":"KEEP","primary_code":null}
{"id":"R0223","corrections":{"E3":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Under frozen P03 E3, whole-inference placement and joint orchestration across a server LLM/VLM and per-drone Grounding DINO models in a multi-UAV system qualify without layer splitting or tensor fusion. Distinct from runtime task directives issued by the server LLM in UTTUT/P3 (Section 3.2), Section 4.2 (p. 5) and Fig. 7 (p. 7) explicitly compare four whole-model placement and orchestration variants: Centralization (server Doubao-1.5-pro orchestrating PPO+ and per-drone Grounding DINO), Parallelism (user concurrently invoking server Doubao-1.5-pro->PPO+ and per-drone Grounding DINO), Distribution (per-drone Grounding DINO->PPO+ without server LLM), and Master (server Doubao-1.5-vision-pro handling vision/control sequentially without per-drone Grounding DINO).","evidence":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0223.pdf","page":1,"locator":"Abstract","quote":"Grounding DINO perception model is deployed at the drone end."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0223.pdf","page":5,"locator":"Section 4.2","quote":"centralization, parallelism, distribution, and master frameworks shown in Figure 7"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0223.pdf","page":7,"locator":"Figure 7 caption","quote":"corresponding to centralized, parallel, distributed, and master frameworks respectively."}],"gap":null},"E4":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Section 4.2 (p. 5) and Table 2 (p. 7) evaluate the four architectural variants (Centralization, Parallelism, Distribution, Master) across two Microsoft AirSim outdoor scenarios (Wind Farm and Residential Courtyard), with each task executed at least 30 times, measuring Success Rate (task completion percentage), R-Time (response time for initial plan generation in seconds), and C-Time (time to complete the task in seconds).","evidence":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0223.pdf","page":5,"locator":"Section 4.2","quote":"Each task is executed at least 30 times"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0223.pdf","page":7,"locator":"Table 2 caption","quote":"Quantitative analysis of Multi-Agent cooperative task planning experiments."}],"gap":null},"E7":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"The actual ACAIB 2025 event publishing notice (held June 6-8, 2025, Nanjing, online) explicitly states that all submissions undergo rigorous review by 2-3 committee experts before publication in the ACM International Conference Proceeding Series (ACM ICPS), corroborated by the ACM ICPS Conference Publication Policy and the 8-page full-text PDF proceedings header (ACM ISBN 979-8-4007-1431-3/2025/06, DOI 10.1145/3760269.3760299, p. 1).","evidence":[{"source":"https://www.ais.cn/attendees/index/FNJRAJ","page":null,"locator":"ACAIB 2025 Publication Notice","quote":"所有的投稿都必须经过2-3位组委会专家审稿"},{"source":"https://www.acm.org/publications/icps/publication-policy","page":null,"locator":"ICPS Conference Publication Policy","quote":"conferences published in the ACM International Conference Proceedings Series (ICPS)."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0223.pdf","page":1,"locator":"ACM Reference Format","quote":"2025 5th International Conference on Automation Control, Algorithm and"}],"gap":null}},"recommendation":"KEEP","primary_code":null}
{"id":"R0292","corrections":{"E3":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Under P03 E3, migrating a complete identified DNN inference workload between UAVs qualifies. Section 4.1 (p. 4) and Section 5.2 (p. 5) show that when Drone D1 has a low battery, active target-tracking responsibility using the identified transformer DNN OSTrack (vitb_256_mae_ce_32x4_ep100) is handed off to Drone D2 via ArUco localization, Wi-Fi template transfer, and ORB feature matching (distinct from OSTrack itself, ORB serves as the inter-UAV target alignment step so D2 can assume continuous OSTrack inference while D1 returns to base).","evidence":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0292.pdf","page":4,"locator":"Section 4.1 Handoff Protocol","quote":"smooth transition of tracking responsibilities between the drones"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0292.pdf","page":4,"locator":"Section 4.1 Handoff Protocol","quote":"enabling D2 to assume the tracking task."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0292.pdf","page":5,"locator":"Section 5.2 UAV Data Handover","quote":"driven by OSTrack with the 'vitb_256_mae_ce_32x4_ep100' configuration."}],"gap":null},"E4":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Section 5.1 (pp. 4-5, Tables 1-2) benchmarks five visual trackers on 5,200 marine aerial frames, and Section 5.2 (pp. 5-6, Table 3, Fig. 5) evaluates two-stage handover template matching across 30/50/70-pixel paddings and conducts 180-second onboard Jetson Nano hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) tests covering OSTrack visual tracking, feature matching, inter-UAV coordination, and ROS-based navigation logic (reporting loop rate, CPU/GPU/memory usage, and power draw); HIL testing satisfies P03 E4 without requiring live physical flight.","evidence":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0292.pdf","page":5,"locator":"Section 5.2 UAV Data Handover","quote":"performed in a hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) environment, which included"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0292.pdf","page":5,"locator":"Section 5.2 UAV Data Handover","quote":"visual tracking, feature matching, inter-UAV coordination, and ROS-based navigation logic"}],"gap":null},"E7":{"status":"UNRESOLVED","finding":"Official MobiSys 2025 and DroNet 2025 pages establish workshop identity (DroNet 2025 co-located with ACM MobiSys 2025) and ACM Conference Publication Policy governs ACM-sponsored venues generally, while DroNet 2025 HotCRP references a program committee and review deadline alongside 6 of 6 submissions accepted. However, workshop identity and institutional repository metadata do not independently establish peer review, and the opened DroNet 2025 CFP omits peer-review criteria while DroNet 2025 SUBMISSIONS/PROGRAM subpages and the ACM DL/Crossref proceedings record for DOI 10.1145/3711875.3737661 were unavailable to verify R0292's specific track and peer-review process.","evidence":[{"source":"https://sites.google.com/view/dronet2025/","page":null,"locator":"DroNet 2025 Homepage / Call for Papers","quote":"Eleventh Workshop on Micro Aerial Vehicle Networks, Systems, and Applications"},{"source":"https://dronet25.hotcrp.com/deadlines","page":null,"locator":"Deadlines","quote":"Submissions must be completed by this deadline to be reviewed."},{"source":"https://dronet25.hotcrp.com/","page":null,"locator":"Conference information","quote":"Deadlines Program committee 6 of 6 submissions accepted"},{"source":"https://www.acm.org/publications/policies/conference-publication-policy","page":null,"locator":"Scope of Policy","quote":"all submitted, accepted, and published articles in ACM Publication venues."}],"gap":"DroNet 2025 official SUBMISSIONS and PROGRAM subpages, ACM Digital Library record (10.1145/3711875.3737661), and Crossref metadata were unavailable; opened DroNet 2025 CFP lacks explicit peer-review criteria and HotCRP shows 6 of 6 submissions accepted without listing accepted papers or review standards."}},"recommendation":"UNRESOLVED","primary_code":null}
{"returned_ids":["R0001","R0113","R0145","R0223","R0292"],"unverified_keys":[{"id":"R0113","criterion":"E3","reason":"Paper claims p. 7 compact navigation embeddings and p. 9 GNN swarm communication with Table 4 ablation, but omits concrete SNN/GNN architectures, embedding/fusion definitions, and experimental linkage to collaborative DNN inference."},{"id":"R0113","criterion":"E7","reason":"AJACCM policy pages self-assert peer review but contradict each other on minimum reviewer count (two vs. one) and contain mismatched journal names (IJDIM/AJMIMC), with no independent publisher/Crossref verification."},{"id":"R0292","criterion":"E7","reason":"ACM DL/Crossref records for 10.1145/3711875.3737661 and DroNet 2025 SUBMISSIONS/PROGRAM subpages were unavailable; opened DroNet 2025 CFP omits review policy and HotCRP reports 6 of 6 submissions accepted without listing papers or review standards."}],"actual_tools":["client:view_file","google:search","google:browse"],"workers_used":false,"worker_assignments":[]}
